## Macros &amp; Functions
<hr class="section-rule lvl-2">

Targets have a complete, auto-generated [API reference]({{_}}/api).  But `compose.mk` also ships a large collection of GNU-make **macros / functions**[^make-functions] that are **not** part of the reflected API reference.  This page collects that side of the library.

!!! tip "Targets vs. Macros, in one line"
    A **target** is a rule you *run* (`./compose.mk io.bash`, or the same
    recursively).  A **macro** is text you *expand* in place: `${name}` (nullary) <!-- docs-code-ok -->
    or `$(call name, arg1, arg2)` (parametric).  Many things exist as **both**. <!-- docs-code-ok -->

### Why macros exist
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

As covered in [Style & Idioms]({{_}}/style), the project *prefers* targets (they compose and reflect better), but reaches for a macro in three situations:

* **Readability:** the `stream.stdin` macro reads better than `cat /dev/stdin`, and the `log` macro better than the equivalent `printf`/`cut` incantation.
* **Performance:** a macro expands in the current shell, avoiding the cost of a recursive `make` call for hot, inner-loop helpers.
* **Scaffolding:** some macros emit *whole or partial makefiles* at parse-time (most the "import" macros).

And by the way, if you're ready to move past vanilla `make` and use the [CMK language]({{_}}/cmk/intro), then [callforms]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callforms) actually *unify* the macro and the target syntax as simple callables, so that you can simply write things like `log(..)` instead.

### Calling conventions
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/callforms.mk', 'demos/cmk/callforms.cmk', github=github, default='left')}}

Above: **nullary** macros are just referenced; **parametric** helpers take arguments through a macro call; and many targets ALSO publish a same-named **macro** (the macro form expands inline, saving a process and avoiding a recursive re-parse; the target form is the equivalent recursive `make` call).  In [cmk-lang]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) (the right-hand tab) the syntax is sugared: a macro-anchored `fn` callform compiles to a `fn` macro call, and a target-anchored `x` callform with a stream compiles to that stream piped into a recursive call of `x` (see the [Quick Reference]({{_}}/cmk/quickref)).

### Inspecting macros
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Two reflection helpers (each is *itself* available as both a target and a macro;
see [Reflection]({{_}}/reflection)) let you inspect any
macro or define at runtime:

```bash {.cli_example}
# Show the raw, unexpanded value of a macro/variable:
$ ./compose.mk mk.get/stream.nl.to.space
xargs

# Dump a `define ... endef` block verbatim
$ ./compose.mk mk.def.read/mk.unpack.kwargs
```

{{site.api_link('mk.get', mkdocs)}} expands a name; {{site.api_link('mk.def.read', mkdocs)}} reads a define block.  Both are the reflection primitives that the importer macros (`import.def` / `import.target`) build on.

